WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:09.140
In part 1, we saw that a recursive function has 3 parts: a base case, a smaller problem, and a combine step.

00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:14.890
And we saw what Python does: every call gets its own frame on the call stack.

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:18.440
Now let's put recursion to work on lists.

00:00:19.233 --> 00:00:21.852
Welcome back to Lecture 6, Recursion.

00:00:21.852 --> 00:00:25.673
This is part 2: recursion on lists, and binary search.

00:00:26.023 --> 00:00:30.259
We'll add up a list and find its smallest number with recursion.

00:00:30.259 --> 00:00:35.223
Then we'll search a sorted list, and see why cutting it in half is so fast.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:39.440
Here's a simple task: add up the numbers in a list.

00:00:39.790 --> 00:00:42.512
Think of it the recursive way.

00:00:42.512 --> 00:00:45.959
What's the sum of the first 0 numbers?

00:00:45.959 --> 00:00:48.590
It's 0. That's our base case.

00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:56.550
And the sum of the first n numbers is the sum of the first n minus 1 numbers, plus the last of them.

00:00:57.150 --> 00:01:01.270
Its index is n minus 1, because Python counts from 0.

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:03.007
Here it is in Python.

00:01:03.007 --> 00:01:05.649
The slides spell the name in camel case.

00:01:05.649 --> 00:01:11.460
We follow the usual Python style, and join the 2 words with an underscore: `linear_sum`.

00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:13.845
If n is 0, return 0.

00:01:13.845 --> 00:01:22.290
Otherwise, return `linear_sum` of data and n minus 1, plus data at index n minus 1.

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:27.059
Run it on this list of 9 numbers, and it prints:

00:01:27.059 --> 00:01:28.440
The sum is 177.

00:01:28.790 --> 00:01:36.594
Each call of `linear_sum` makes at most 1 recursive call, so the calls form a single chain.

00:01:36.594 --> 00:01:39.252
That's called linear recursion.

00:01:39.252 --> 00:01:41.310
Factorial is linear too.

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:44.727
Pause the video and predict.

00:01:44.727 --> 00:01:51.952
For the list 4, 3, 6, 2, 8, which calls does `linear_sum` of data and 5 make?

00:01:51.952 --> 00:01:57.300
Which call returns first, and what does each call return?

00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:00.933
The calls go down from 5 to 0.

00:02:00.933 --> 00:02:05.969
The call with n equal to 0 returns first, with 0.

00:02:05.969 --> 00:02:11.210
Then the calls return 4, 7, 13, 15, and finally 23.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:15.040
Now let's watch these calls on the call stack.

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:21.485
`linear_sum` with n equal to 5 needs the sum of the first 4 numbers, so it waits.

00:02:21.485 --> 00:02:26.150
That call needs the first 3, and so on, all the way down to 0.

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:33.740
Now 6 frames are on the stack, and each frame has its own n, from 5 down to 0.

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:35.782
The base case returns 0.

00:02:35.782 --> 00:02:39.730
Then each call adds its own element, on the way back up.

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:42.327
0 plus 4 is 4.

00:02:42.327 --> 00:02:44.574
4 plus 3 is 7.

00:02:44.574 --> 00:02:46.982
7 plus 6 is 13.

00:02:46.982 --> 00:02:49.550
13 plus 2 is 15.

00:02:49.550 --> 00:02:52.760
And 15 plus 8 is 23.

00:02:53.110 --> 00:02:58.504
Notice that the adding happens after the smaller call returns.

00:02:58.504 --> 00:03:02.070
On the way down, nothing is added at all.

00:03:02.867 --> 00:03:06.507
There's another way to write the same idea, with slicing.

00:03:06.857 --> 00:03:08.738
An empty list sums to 0.

00:03:08.738 --> 00:03:14.848
Otherwise, the sum is the first element, plus the sum of the rest of the list.

00:03:14.848 --> 00:03:18.217
The slice from index 1 onwards is the rest.

00:03:18.567 --> 00:03:20.403
This reads more naturally.

00:03:20.403 --> 00:03:25.767
But every slice makes a copy of the rest of the list, so it does extra work.

00:03:26.117 --> 00:03:29.659
This shape is worth a name: first and rest.

00:03:29.659 --> 00:03:33.201
Handle the empty sequence as the base case.

00:03:33.201 --> 00:03:38.637
Otherwise, combine the first element with the answer for the rest.

00:03:39.237 --> 00:03:46.797
Sum, count, maximum, does it contain this value, reverse: they all fit this pattern.

00:03:47.567 --> 00:03:50.967
Let's use the pattern to find the smallest number in a list.

00:03:51.317 --> 00:03:54.592
This time the base case is a list with 1 element.

00:03:54.592 --> 00:03:56.997
Its smallest number is that element.

00:03:57.347 --> 00:04:03.507
Otherwise, find the minimum of the rest with a recursive call, and store it in `rest_min`.

00:04:03.857 --> 00:04:08.857
Then return whichever is smaller: the first element, or `rest_min`.

00:04:09.207 --> 00:04:15.907
For 8, 3, 9, 1, 7, the calls go down to the list that holds just 7.

00:04:15.907 --> 00:04:21.007
Coming back up, they return 7, then 1, 1, 1, and 1.

00:04:21.007 --> 00:04:22.607
The answer is 1.

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:25.185
Now a new problem.

00:04:25.185 --> 00:04:27.960
Is a target value in a list?

00:04:28.310 --> 00:04:32.642
If the list is in no particular order, there's only 1 way.

00:04:32.642 --> 00:04:37.123
Look at every element in turn, until you find it or run out.

00:04:37.123 --> 00:04:38.990
That's sequential search.

00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:44.020
In the worst case, when the target isn't there, it looks at every element.

00:04:44.370 --> 00:04:47.577
But if the list is sorted, we can do much better.

00:04:47.577 --> 00:04:49.409
Think of a paper dictionary.

00:04:49.409 --> 00:04:54.840
You open it near the middle, see whether your word comes before or after that page,

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:57.850
and throw away the half that can't contain it.

00:04:58.634 --> 00:05:00.299
That's binary search.

00:05:00.299 --> 00:05:03.234
It keeps 2 indices, `low` and `high`.

00:05:03.584 --> 00:05:09.250
Every element that could still be the target has an index between `low` and `high`.

00:05:09.250 --> 00:05:11.229
We call these the candidates.

00:05:11.229 --> 00:05:14.984
At the start, `low` is 0, and `high` is the last index.

00:05:15.334 --> 00:05:19.208
If `low` is greater than `high`, no candidates are left.

00:05:19.208 --> 00:05:21.214
The target isn't in the list.

00:05:21.564 --> 00:05:24.793
Otherwise, look at the middle candidate.

00:05:24.793 --> 00:05:30.444
Its index, `mid`, is `low` plus `high`, divided by 2 and rounded down.

00:05:30.794 --> 00:05:34.902
If the target equals the middle element, we've found it.

00:05:34.902 --> 00:05:41.652
If the target is smaller, it can only be on the left, so search from `low` to `mid` minus 1.

00:05:41.652 --> 00:05:45.394
If it's bigger, search from `mid` plus 1 to `high`.

00:05:45.994 --> 00:05:50.415
Searching 1 half is the same problem, on a smaller range.

00:05:50.415 --> 00:05:52.354
So the code is recursive.

00:05:52.704 --> 00:05:56.439
Notice that it returns `True` or `False`, and doesn't print.

00:05:56.439 --> 00:06:00.984
The caller decides what to print, so the function can be reused anywhere.

00:06:01.767 --> 00:06:05.607
Let's search for 6 in this sorted list of 9 numbers.

00:06:05.957 --> 00:06:10.211
`low` is 0 and `high` is 8, so `mid` is 4.

00:06:10.211 --> 00:06:12.743
The middle element is 16.

00:06:12.743 --> 00:06:16.694
6 is smaller, so we keep the left part:

00:06:16.694 --> 00:06:18.517
`low` 0, `high` 3.

00:06:18.867 --> 00:06:22.152
`mid` is 1, and the element is 3.

00:06:22.152 --> 00:06:26.035
6 is bigger, so we keep the right part:

00:06:26.035 --> 00:06:27.827
`low` 2, `high` 3.

00:06:28.177 --> 00:06:31.499
`mid` is 2, and 5 is smaller than 6.

00:06:31.499 --> 00:06:35.097
So `low` becomes 3, and `high` stays 3.

00:06:35.447 --> 00:06:38.898
`mid` is 3, and the element is 6.

00:06:38.898 --> 00:06:41.407
We found it, in 4 steps.

00:06:41.757 --> 00:06:43.092
Now search for 7.

00:06:43.092 --> 00:06:47.648
The steps are the same, until `low` and `high` are both 3.

00:06:47.648 --> 00:06:52.597
6 is smaller than 7, so `low` becomes 4, and `high` is still 3.

00:06:52.947 --> 00:06:55.654
`low` is greater than `high`.

00:06:55.654 --> 00:07:00.227
No candidates are left, so the answer is `False`.

00:07:00.227 --> 00:07:04.987
7 would sit between 6 and 16, but nothing is there.

00:07:05.767 --> 00:07:07.946
Why is binary search so fast?

00:07:07.946 --> 00:07:13.207
Each call either finds the target, or throws away half the candidates.

00:07:13.557 --> 00:07:16.017
Start with 1,000,000 elements.

00:07:16.017 --> 00:07:18.559
After 1 step, 500,000 are left.

00:07:18.559 --> 00:07:19.625
Then 250,000.

00:07:19.625 --> 00:07:26.677
After about 20 halvings, just 1 is left, because 2 to the power 20 is about 1,000,000.

00:07:27.027 --> 00:07:31.030
Sequential search might need 1,000,000 comparisons.

00:07:31.030 --> 00:07:33.307
Binary search needs about 20.

00:07:33.907 --> 00:07:40.587
In Lecture 9, you'll learn to write this as big O of log n, versus big O of n.

00:07:41.367 --> 00:07:42.846
But there's a catch.

00:07:42.846 --> 00:07:48.687
Binary search on unsorted data gives wrong answers, and it gives them silently.

00:07:49.037 --> 00:07:52.964
Search for 1 in the list 5, 1, 9, 3, 7.

00:07:52.964 --> 00:07:55.381
The middle element is 9.

00:07:55.381 --> 00:08:00.517
1 is smaller, so binary search keeps the left part.

00:08:00.867 --> 00:08:02.354
Now the middle is 5.

00:08:02.354 --> 00:08:07.707
1 is smaller again, so it looks to the left of 5, where there's nothing.

00:08:08.057 --> 00:08:13.568
It returns `False`, even though 1 is right there, next to 5.

00:08:13.568 --> 00:08:16.324
No error, just a wrong answer.

00:08:16.324 --> 00:08:20.457
Binary search is only correct on sorted data.

00:08:21.234 --> 00:08:22.474
Let's sum up.

00:08:22.824 --> 00:08:27.254
Linear recursion makes at most 1 recursive call per call.

00:08:27.254 --> 00:08:30.984
On lists, it follows the pattern first and rest.

00:08:31.334 --> 00:08:36.694
Binary search keeps `low` and `high`, and halves the candidates at every call.

00:08:37.044 --> 00:08:40.844
It's very fast, but only correct on a sorted list.

00:08:41.194 --> 00:08:42.540
Here's one to try.

00:08:42.540 --> 00:08:50.314
Change binary search so that it returns the index of the target, or minus one if the target isn't there.

00:08:50.864 --> 00:08:53.673
In part 3, we'll compute powers much faster,

00:08:53.673 --> 00:08:57.184
and meet functions that call themselves more than once.

